.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt PAZARID.TOML 5
.Os pazari
.Sh NAME
.Nm pazarid.toml
.Nd configuration file for the pazari host memory daemon
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr pazarid 1
reads one TOML file, by default
.Pa /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml .
.Pp
.Nm pazarid Cm example-config
prints an annotated file carrying every key at its default.
A copy is installed with the package as
.Pa share/doc/pazari/examples/pazarid.toml .
.Pp
Sizes and durations are written as in
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5 :
a size is a bare integer or a number with a unit, where
.Cm MiB
counts 1024 and
.Cm MB
counts 1000; a duration is always a string, and unless a key says otherwise it
must be greater than zero and at most
.Cm 24h .
.Sh WHAT THE DAEMON DECIDES
The daemon decides whether a guest may grow, how large its memory device
should be made, and which guest to ask for memory back when the host itself is
short.
.Pp
It does not take memory from a guest.
The host may send a reclaim request, and a guest that does not want to answer
one keeps what it holds until its ceiling.
There is no mechanism in the daemon that unplugs memory a guest has not agreed
to give up; the ceiling is the only hard bound, and it bounds growth rather
than what is already held.
.Sh SETTINGS
.Ss General
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm tick_interval No = Ar duration
How often the daemon re-reads the host's memory and sweeps what it knows about
its guests: reconciling actuations, noticing guests that have gone quiet,
expiring reclaim deadlines, and refreshing its gauges.
The default is
.Cm 1s .
.Pp
This is the resolution of the daemon's whole view of the host.
Nothing here bounds how fast a grant is answered; a request is decided as it
arrives.
.El
.Ss policy
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm host_floor No = Ar size
Memory the host keeps for itself: the hypervisor's own working set, the daemon,
the storage stack, and whatever else shares the host.
Required; zero is accepted.
.Pp
It is what the arbiter refuses to grant past, and it is counted into the
startup warning.
A grow is granted only if what the host has, less everything already promised
to other guests and less what this grow would take, still leaves the floor.
Equality with the floor is granted.
.It Cm welcome_status_interval No = Ar duration
How often a guest must send
.Cm status .
The default is
.Cm 1s .
.Pp
This is sent to the guest in
.Cm welcome
and is the unit of the staleness window, so shortening it makes the daemon
notice a departed guest sooner and makes every guest talk more.
.It Cm grant_min_interval No = Ar duration
How long a guest waits after a grant before another one.
The default is
.Cm 5s .
.Pp
A grow that arrives sooner is refused with
.Cm rate_limited
and the remaining time as
.Va retry_after_ms .
It is also the retry advice the daemon gives on a
.Cm host_pressure
refusal.
It rate-limits grows only; an offer to release is never refused for asking too
often.
.It Cm reconcile_deadline No = Ar duration
How long an actuation is given to apply before the daemon stops waiting for it.
The default is
.Cm 30s .
.Pp
When it passes, a grow is accepted at whatever size the device reached and a
shrink is logged as having missed.
Set it above the time a resize of the largest step a guest can take actually
costs on this host; a virtio-mem resize is not instant, and a deadline below
the cost turns every grow into a reconciliation failure.
.It Cm shrink_revert No = Ar boolean
Whether a shrink that did not apply is taken back to the size the guest still
has.
The default is
.Cm true .
.Pp
Without this a shrink that stalls leaves the device asking for a size the guest
never reached, so the guest goes on holding memory the daemon has stopped
counting against it.
.It Cm stale_after No = Ar count
Missed
.Cm status
intervals before a guest is marked stale.
Must be greater than zero; the default is
.Cm 3 .
.Pp
The window is
.Cm welcome_status_interval
multiplied by this.
A stale guest is still connected and still holds what it holds; the mark is a
signal to an operator, and to the
.Va pazarid_guest_stale
gauge, that the daemon's view of that guest has stopped being refreshed.
.It Cm reclaim_deadline No = Ar duration
How long a guest has to answer a reclaim request.
The default is
.Cm 30s .
.Pp
A guest with a request outstanding is left alone until it answers or the
deadline runs out, and a guest that refuses is left alone until the deadline
would have passed.
Too short and a guest that is genuinely reclaiming gets asked again while it
works; too long and one unresponsive guest holds up the sweep.
.It Cm grant_swap No = Ar boolean
Whether free host swap counts towards what a grow may be granted out of.
The default is
.Cm false .
.Pp
With
.Cm false
a grant promises RAM: the daemon grants against
.Va MemAvailable
alone.
With
.Cm true
it grants against
.Va MemAvailable
plus
.Va SwapFree ,
and is deliberately lending out memory the host may have to page for.
Either way, until the relevant field has been read every grow is refused with
.Cm host_pressure .
.Pp
This pairs naturally with the
.Sx policy.revocation
table:
a host that lends out swap should be able to ask for it back.
It pairs badly with zram, whose
.Va SwapFree
counts what the device would hold uncompressed rather than what it will cost in
RAM, and worst of all for the incompressible pages a memory-hungry workload
tends to produce.
The daemon warns at startup when it finds zram in
.Pa /proc/swaps
under this setting.
.El
.Ss policy.revocation
How the host takes memory back when it comes under pressure itself.
Leave the table out and the daemon never asks a guest for memory back on its
own initiative.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm psi No = Ar percent
The host memory pressure, as
.Va some avg10
from
.Pa /proc/pressure/memory
in percent of wall time, at which the daemon starts asking guests for memory
back.
Greater than zero and at most
.Cm 100 ;
required when the table is present.
.It Cm step No = Ar size
How much memory one such request asks a guest for.
Must be greater than zero; the default is
.Cm 256MiB .
.El
.Pp
On every tick at or over
.Cm psi
the daemon sends one reclaim request to the guest holding the most over its
boot memory, asking for
.Cm step
bytes or for what that guest holds over boot, whichever is less.
One guest is asked per tick.
.Pp
A host asked to revoke must be able to measure pressure.
Where this table is present and the kernel carries no
.Pa /proc/pressure/memory ,
the daemon refuses to start.
.Ss metrics
Leave the table out and the daemon binds no listener and keeps no counters.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm listen No = Ar address
The address the Prometheus endpoint answers scrapes on, for example
.Ql 127.0.0.1:9650 .
Required when the table is present.
.Pp
Any
.Cm GET ,
on any path, is answered with the OpenMetrics text exposition; any other method
is answered with
.Cm 405 .
See
.Sx METRICS .
.El
.Ss guests.<name>
One table per guest.
The name is the daemon's own; it is what appears in the logs and in the
.Va guest
label of every per-guest series, and it need not be the guest's hostname.
At least one guest table is required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm ceiling No = Ar size
The most memory this guest may hold, counted the way the guest counts its own
memory: boot memory plus whatever is plugged.
Must be greater than zero; required.
.Pp
It is sent to the guest in
.Cm welcome ,
and every grant target is clamped to it.
A request that buys the guest nothing against it is refused with
.Cm ceiling .
The ceiling may be lowered at run time by editing the file and restarting the
daemon, which will tell the guest its new ceiling and then ask for the
difference back; it does not take the memory.
.El
.Ss guests.<name>.transport
How this guest reaches the daemon.
Required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqvsock\(dq
.Dv AF_VSOCK .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm cid No = Ar id
The context id the guest dials from.
This identifies the guest rather than naming an address to bind; the listener
is bound at
.Dv VMADDR_CID_ANY .
Required.
.It Cm port No = Ar port
The port the guest dials.
The default is
.Cm 4650 .
One listener is bound per distinct port across all guests.
.Pp
Ports at or below 1023 are reserved, and binding one wants
.Dv CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE ,
which the packaged daemon does not have.
.El
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqunix\(dq
An
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm path No = Ar path
The socket this guest dials.
Required.
A stale socket file at that path is removed before the listener is bound.
.El
.El
.Pp
Two guests may not share a context id or a socket path; the daemon refuses to
start if they do, naming the peer and the guest that already holds it.
.Ss guests.<name>.qmp
The QMP socket of the hypervisor process running this guest, and the memory
device on it.
Required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm path No = Ar path
The QMP socket of the qemu process running the guest.
Required.
.Pp
Give the daemon a socket of its own rather than the one the VM manager already
drives; see
.Xr pazari 7 .
.It Cm device No = Ar id
The
.Va id=
of the guest's virtio-mem device.
Required.
.Pp
The daemon addresses it as
.Pa /machine/peripheral/<id>
and refuses to attach if the device is missing or is not a virtio-mem.
.It Cm deadline No = Ar duration
How long one QMP command is given to finish.
The default is
.Cm 5s .
.It Cm retry_min No = Ar duration
The first delay before a device that did not answer is opened again.
The default is
.Cm 500ms .
.It Cm retry_max No = Ar duration
The cap that delay doubles towards.
Must not be less than
.Cm retry_min ;
the default is
.Cm 30s .
.Pp
The backoff covers opening the device, reading its size at attach, and watching
it for changes.
It does not cover a resize the device refused: that is counted, logged, and
left to reconciliation, and the connection is kept.
.Pp
A guest whose device has not answered yet is not negotiated with.
.El
.Ss guests.<name>.qmp.mode
How the daemon uses the QMP socket.
The table is optional and defaults to
.Cm held .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqheld\(dq
One connection held open for the life of the device.
Sizes come from the hypervisor's own size-change events, so the daemon learns
that a resize has landed as soon as it has.
Takes no other key.
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqper_operation\(dq
A fresh connection per command, for a socket shared with other tools.
No events are subscribed to, so the size is polled instead.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm poll No = Ar duration
How often the size is resampled while a resize is being processed.
The default is
.Cm 1s .
.Pp
This is the resolution of every resize under this mode, and it is added to
every grow round trip.
.El
.El
.Sh CAPACITY
.Ss Provisioning
At startup the daemon compares the guests' ceilings and
.Cm host_floor
against what the host holds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sum(ceilings) + host_floor > MemTotal + SwapTotal
.Ed
.Pp
If that holds, it warns once and starts anyway.
Over-provisioning is a legitimate posture: the point of negotiation is that
guests do not all want their ceiling at once.
.Pp
Operators who want the strong guarantee can have it through how they set
ceilings rather than by asking the daemon to enforce it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sum(ceilings) + host_floor <= MemTotal
.Ed
.Pp
Every byte any guest could ever hold is then RAM.
.Ss Per-guest host-side limits
Nothing in the daemon bounds any one guest's share of the host.
Under
.Cm grant_swap No = Cm true
in particular, one greedy guest can take the free swap into its own pages and
leave the others paging for it.
.Pp
Put a host-side limit on each VM's own scope or service, so that a guest's
appetite is charged to that guest.
.Sh STARTUP CHECKS
Before it serves anything, the daemon reads
.Pa /proc/meminfo
and fails to start if it cannot.
It then:
.Bl -bullet -width Ds
.It
warns once if the guests at their ceilings plus
.Cm host_floor
come to more than the host holds in RAM and swap together;
.It
refuses to start if
.Sx policy.revocation
is present and the kernel carries no
.Pa /proc/pressure/memory ;
.It
warns, under
.Cm grant_swap No = Cm true ,
if
.Pa /proc/swaps
names a zram device.
.El
.Sh METRICS
With a
.Sx metrics
table present, the daemon publishes, under the prefix
.Va pazarid :
.Pp
Per-guest gauges, each labelled
.Va guest :
.Va guest_size_bytes ,
what the guest's memory device holds;
.Va guest_ceiling_bytes ;
.Va guest_connected ,
1 while the guest is connected and past the handshake;
.Va guest_stale ,
1 while it has gone quiet; and
.Va guest_reclaim_outstanding ,
1 while a reclaim request is waiting on it.
.Pp
Host gauges:
.Va host_available_bytes
and
.Va host_swap_free_bytes ,
as the daemon last read them, and
.Va host_psi ,
the host's memory
.Va some avg10 .
.Pp
Counters:
.Va connections_total ,
.Va disconnections_total ,
.Va closes_total ,
.Va grants_total ,
.Va reclaim_requests_total ,
.Va denies_total
with a
.Va reason
label,
.Va guest_messages_total
with a
.Va kind
label naming the protocol message, and, per guest,
.Va device_reconnects_total ,
.Va device_seed_failures_total
and
.Va device_refused_actuations_total .
.Pp
The daemon refreshes its gauges once per
.Cm tick_interval .
.Pp
.Va device_refused_actuations_total
counts resizes the hypervisor would not make.
A device configured with a block size the daemon's targets do not divide by
looks like this from here.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
The default configuration file.
.It Pa share/doc/pazari/examples/pazarid.toml
An annotated file carrying every key at its default.
.It Pa /proc/meminfo
Read every tick for
.Va MemTotal , MemAvailable , SwapTotal
and
.Va SwapFree .
.It Pa /proc/pressure/memory
Read every tick for
.Va some avg10 .
Absent is tolerated unless
.Sx policy.revocation
is configured.
.It Pa /proc/swaps
Read once at startup, under
.Cm grant_swap No = Cm true ,
to look for zram.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A host keeping four gigabytes for itself, taking memory back when it comes
under pressure, and answering to one guest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tick_interval = "1s"

[policy]
host_floor = "4GiB"
grant_min_interval = "5s"
grant_swap = false

[policy.revocation]
psi = 20.0
step = "256MiB"

[metrics]
listen = "127.0.0.1:9650"

[guests.example]
ceiling = "32GiB"

[guests.example.transport]
kind = "vsock"
cid = 3

[guests.example.qmp]
path = "/run/pazari/example.qmp"
device = "vm0"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pazari-agent 1 ,
.Xr pazarid 1 ,
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5 ,
.Xr pazari 7
.Pp
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr qemu 1
